<DocsLayout
  title="Knowledge base"
  description="How to load a PDF résumé into Mockingbird, how the knowledge base affects LLM answers and how to refresh the context."
  locale="en"
>
  <h1>Knowledge base</h1>
  <p>
    <strong>In short:</strong> the knowledge base is the context the LLM relies on
    when building answers. In Mockingbird it's usually a PDF résumé and technology
    notes. The file is processed locally and never sent anywhere.
  </p>

  <h2>Loading a résumé</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the <strong>Résumé</strong> tab.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Upload PDF</strong> and pick a file.</li>
    <li>Wait for indexing — the app shows the number of chunks.</li>
  </ol>
  <blockquote>
    The résumé is processed locally via <code>pypdf</code> — the file is never uploaded anywhere.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>How it affects answers</h2>
  <p>
    When the interviewer asks a question, relevant knowledge base chunks are mixed
    into the LLM context. The more precise and structured your résumé, the more
    specific the answers.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>List technologies and projects with details (what you did, with which tools).</li>
    <li>Split it into sections: <em>Experience</em>, <em>Stack</em>, <em>Projects</em>.</li>
    <li>Avoid scanned images without a text layer.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Refreshing the knowledge</h2>
  <p>
    To replace the context — upload a new PDF again: the old chunks are re-indexed.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
